## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design consistent, beginner-friendly multi-interface APIs with shared auth and error models, reflecting real-world needs at large platforms.

## Key Concepts

- Unified versioning across REST, GraphQL, gRPC
- JWT-based authentication with common claims (aud, iss, tenant, roles)
- Consistent error payloads across interfaces
- Documentation and discovery considerations (OpenAPI support parity)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Unified error payloads
class ApiError extends Error { constructor(code, message, details){ super(message); this.code = code; this.details = details; } }

// Pseudo REST handler
function getProducts(req, res){ if(!req.user) return res.status(401).json({code:'UNAUTH', message:'Auth required'}); res.json({items: []}); }

// Pseudo GraphQL resolver
async function productsResolver(ctx){ if(!ctx.user) throw new ApiError('UNAUTH','Auth required',{}); return { id:'p1' }; }

// Pseudo gRPC handler
function GetProducts(call, callback){ const md = call.metadata; if(!md || !md.get('authorization')) return callback({ code: grpc.status.UNAUTHENTICATED, message: 'Auth required' }); callback(null, { products: [] }); }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle token revocation and rotation across all three interfaces?
- What testing strategy ensures parity of behavior across REST, GraphQL, and gRPC?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to design scalable, multi-tenant storage systems with zero-downtime operational capabilities.

## Key Concepts
- **Sharding Strategy**: Tenant_id-based consistent hashing with range partitions to ensure even distribution and minimize hotspots
- **Replication Topology**: Multi-region 3-way replication with leader-follower consensus for durability and availability
- **Online Rebalancing**: Versioned migration system with dual-write periods and gradual traffic shifting
- **Isolation & Security**: Per-tenant namespaces with dedicated access controls and resource quotas

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: migration plan snippet
async function migrateShard(oldShard, newShard) {
  await startTransfer(oldShard, newShard);
  await verifyIntegrity(oldShard, newShard);
  await switchTraffic(oldShard, newShard);
  await completeMigration(oldShard);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design multi-tenant trust with dynamic cryptography choices and mitigate cache stampede while enforcing tenant boundaries across OIDC and SAML.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant algorithm policy
- Key rotation with stampede controls
- JWKS caching and invalidation
- Replay protection for SAML
- Tenant-scoped claims in internal JWTs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: verify token by kid
function verifyAndIssue(idToken, tenantId){
  const kid = idToken.header.kid;
  const key = keyCache.get(tenantId, kid);
  if(!key) throw new Error("Unknown key");
  const payload = verifyJWT(idToken, key, {alg: key.alg, iss: tenantIss(tenantId)});
  const internal = signJWT({sub: payload.sub, tenant: tenantId, scopes: payload.scopes}, internalKey);
  return internal;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test key rotation safety and replay protection?
- How would you configure per-tenant defaults and overrides in a config store?

